export default {"id":"m4l-level-4","number":4,"title":"Module 4","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"4.1","modules":[{"id":"m4l-m-4-1-1","code":"4.1","title":"4.1","blurb":"4.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-4-1-1","title":"4.1.1: Audio Device Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 4.1.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 4 / Section 4.1","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 3.3.1 - DSP Voice Architecture and Multichannel Awareness.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Section 31.3 (<em>Creating Audio Effects</em>).","Cycling '74: <em>Creating Audio Effect Devices in Max for Live</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Audio Input and Output Routing</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Explain the architectural role of <code>plugin~</code> and <code>plugout~</code> as the primary host audio boundary objects in Ableton Live audio effect devices.","Route stereo audio streams symmetrically through a device to prevent silent channel bugs and phase asymmetry.","Manage the real-time audio audition state using the <strong>Live Preview</strong> toggle during active patch editing.","Understand how host latency compensation interacts with the device via the <strong>Defined Latency</strong> patcher attribute.","Construct a functional stereo pass-through audio utility with dry/wet crossfading."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 The Audio Effect Chain Boundary","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"An Ableton Live Max Audio Effect exists as an in-line processor within an audio track or post-instrument signal chain. Audio data enters and exits the patcher graph through two dedicated host-interface objects:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            HOST AUDIO ROUTING GRAPH                           |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  Live Track Signal Flow:                                                      |\n|  [ Upstream Device / Audio Clip ]                                             |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               | (Left &amp; Right 64-bit Audio Stream)                            |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | Max Audio Effect (.amxd)                                                |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |      plugin~      |  &lt;-- Ingests stereo audio from Live track        |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            | (Internal MSP Signal Graph)                                |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |  DSP Processing   |                                                  |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |     plugout~      |  &lt;-- Returns stereo audio to Live track          |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  [ Downstream Device / Track Mixer ]                                          |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>[plugin~]</code>:</strong> Ingests the left (outlet 1) and right (outlet 2) audio channels from the track insertion point.","<strong><code>[plugout~]</code>:</strong> Transmits the processed left (inlet 1) and right (inlet 2) audio signals back to Live's mixing chain."],"ordered":false},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Stereo Channel Discipline and Silent Channel Bugs","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton Live tracks operate natively in stereo. When designing Max Audio Effects, channel symmetry must be strictly maintained:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mono Processing Error:</strong> Connecting only outlet 1 of <code>[plugin~]</code> to both inlets of <code>[plugout~]</code> collapses the stereo image to mono, discarding all stereo information from upstream devices.","<strong>Single-Channel Dropout Error:</strong> Connecting processed audio to inlet 1 of <code>[plugout~]</code> while leaving inlet 2 unconnected causes the right channel of the track to drop completely silent.","<strong>True Stereo Topology:</strong> Always maintain independent left and right processing paths unless an explicit mono summing or balance algorithm is intended by design."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Defined Latency and Host Delay Compensation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If an audio effect employs algorithmic lookahead, high-order linear-phase filtering, or FFT windowing, it introduces internal processing latency:"},{"type":"list","items":["In standalone Max, latency must be manually compensated.","In Max for Live, the developer declares this latency using the <strong>Defined Latency</strong> attribute in the <strong>Patcher Inspector</strong>.","Ableton Live reads this attribute and automatically delays all other tracks in the Live Set by the declared sample duration to maintain phase-accurate synchronization across the mix."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>(Note: Advanced latency declaration and testing are covered in detail in Lesson 9.3.1).</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Create a professional stereo gain utility with an equal-power dry/wet crossfader and independent channel mute switches using <code>[plugin~]</code> and <code>[plugout~]</code>."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, drag a default <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> onto an Audio Track with playing audio.","Select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Inspect the default template canvas. Locate <code>[plugin~]</code> at the top and <code>[plugout~]</code> at the bottom.","Delete any generic placeholder controls between <code>[plugin~]</code> and <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Create two <code>[*~ 1.0]</code> multiplier objects: one for Channel 1 (Left) and one for Channel 2 (Right).","Connect outlet 1 of <code>[plugin~]</code> to inlet 1 of <code>[*~ 1.0]</code> (Left).","Connect outlet 2 of <code>[plugin~]</code> to inlet 1 of <code>[*~ 1.0]</code> (Right).","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>Gain_Control</code> (Float, range: <code>0.0 to 2.0</code>, default: <code>1.0</code>).","Connect <code>[live.dial]</code> through a <code>[1 20(</code> message box\\rightarrow$ <code>[line~ 1.0]</code>.","Connect the output of <code>[line~ 1.0]</code> to the right (gain) inlet of both left and right <code>[*~]</code> multipliers.","Connect the left multiplier outlet to inlet 1 of <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Connect the right multiplier outlet to inlet 2 of <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Save the device as <code>Stereo_Gain_Utility.amxd</code> in your User Library.","In Live, verify that adjusting <code>Gain_Control</code> scales both channels equally and click-free."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  +---------------------------+\n                  |          plugin~          |\n                  +-------------+-------------+\n                  (Left Audio)  |             | (Right Audio)\n                                |             |\n              +-----------------+             +-----------------+\n              |                                                 |\n              v                                                 v\n        +-----------+                                     +-----------+\n        |   *~ 1.0  |                                     |   *~ 1.0  |\n        +-----+-----+                                     +-----+-----+\n              ^                                                 ^\n              |               +-----------------+               |\n              +===============|    line~ 1.0    |===============+\n                              +--------+--------+\n                                       ^\n                                       |\n                                +------+------+\n                                |    $1 20    |\n                                +------+------+\n                                       ^\n                                       |\n                                +------+------+\n                                |  live.dial  |\n                                +-------------+\n              |                                                 |\n              v (Left Out)                                      v (Right Out)\n        +-----+-------------------------------------------------+-----+\n        |                          plugout~                           |\n        +-------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Summing to Mono Accidentally:</strong> Routing both outlets of <code>[plugin~]</code> into a single <code>[+~]</code> object and sending that output to both inlets of <code>[plugout~]</code>. This strips all spatial width from the track.","<strong>Forgetting <code>line~</code> on Master Gain:</strong> Controlling gain directly with message floats into <code>[*~]</code>. When automated in Live, this creates severe digital zipper noise.","<strong>Placing <code>midiin</code> in an Audio Effect:</strong> Attempting to use MIDI input objects in a standard Max Audio Effect without establishing sidechain routing. Audio effects receive audio streams directly; MIDI processing requires the Max MIDI Effect or Instrument template."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the signal flow from an Ableton audio track, through <code>[plugin~]</code>, through an internal DSP chain, and back to the track via <code>[plugout~]</code>.","[ ] Identify the cause of a bug where an audio track plays back only in the left monitor speaker.","[ ] Explain the function of Live's delay compensation engine when a Max Audio Effect declares latency."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Section 31.3: \"Creating Audio Effect Devices\", pp. 583–585. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Creating Audio Effect Devices in Max for Live</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Audio Chain Architecture</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-4-2-2","code":"4.2","title":"4.2","blurb":"4.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-4-2-1","title":"4.2.1: MIDI Device Architecture and Flow","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 4.2.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 4 / Section 4.2","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 4.1.1 - Audio Device Architecture (<code>plugin~</code> and <code>plugout~</code>).md).","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Creating MIDI Effect Devices in Max for Live</em>.","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Section 31.3 (<em>Creating MIDI Effects</em>).","Ableton Knowledge Base: <em>Max for Live specific objects</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Explain the architectural role of <code>midiin</code> and <code>midiout</code> as host MIDI stream boundary objects.","Differentiate between standard raw MIDI byte streams and high-level parsed messages (<code>notein</code>/<code>noteout</code>, <code>ctlin</code>/<code>ctlout</code>).","Implement the three primary MIDI effect design patterns: <strong>Thru (passthrough)</strong>, <strong>Transform (process/reshape)</strong>, and <strong>Block (filter)</strong>.","Construct channel filtering and pitch transposition routines for real-time track execution.","Maintain proper device chain positioning rules (MIDI effects placed prior to instruments on MIDI tracks)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 The MIDI Device Chain Boundary","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A Max MIDI Effect operates exclusively in the message domain on Live MIDI tracks, positioned <strong>before</strong> any virtual instrument or external MIDI output:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            HOST MIDI ROUTING GRAPH                            |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  Live Track Signal Flow:                                                      |\n|  [ MIDI Clip / External Keyboard Controller ]                                 |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               | (Real-time MIDI Event Stream)                                 |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | Max MIDI Effect (.amxd)                                                 |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |      midiin       |  &lt;-- Ingests raw serial MIDI stream from Live    |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            | (Raw byte stream / Parsed messages)                        |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |  MIDI Processing  |                                                  |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |      midiout      |  &lt;-- Returns processed MIDI stream to Live       |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  [ Instrument (e.g. Drift, Operator, Max Instrument) / MIDI Out ]             |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Raw Stream vs. High-Level Parsing","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"In Max for Live MIDI devices, two complementary approaches exist for receiving and transmitting MIDI:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        MIDI INGESTION PATTERNS                                    |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+\n| Pattern           | Objects                           | Appropriate Use Case      |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+\n| **High-Level**    | `[notein]` / `[noteout]`,         | Processing standard notes |\n| **Parsing**       | `[ctlin]` / `[ctlout]`,           | (pitch, velocity), CC     |\n|                   | `[pgmin]` / `[pgmout]`            | automation, or program    |\n|                   |                                   | changes with clear typing.|\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+\n| **Raw Stream**    | `[midiin]` / `[midiout]`,         | Full stream passthrough,  |\n| **Handling**      | `[midiparse]` / `[midiformat]`    | pitch bend, aftertouch,   |\n|                   |                                   | and MPE expression data.  |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.3 The Three Core MIDI Processing Patterns","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When authoring MIDI effects, every message type must be handled according to one of three architectural patterns:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">1. Thru Pattern (Passthrough untouched):\n   [midiin] -----------------------------------------------------&gt; [midiout]\n\n2. Transform Pattern (Reshape payload):\n   [notein] ---&gt; [Pitch + 12] ---&gt; [Velocity * 0.8] ---&gt; [noteout]\n\n3. Block Pattern (Conditional filter):\n   [notein] ---&gt; [split 36 84] (Passes only valid range) ---&gt; [noteout]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Note Tuple Handling and Note-Off Integrity","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In MIDI protocol, every sounding note requires a matching <strong>Note-Off</strong> event (or Note-On with velocity <code>0</code>):"},{"type":"list","items":["If an effect alters the pitch of a Note-On event from <code>60</code> to <code>64</code>, it <strong>must</strong> also alter the corresponding Note-Off pitch from <code>60</code> to <code>64</code>.","Transposing a Note-On while failing to transpose the Note-Off leaves note <code>60</code> stuck on indefinitely, creating a severe performance bug (\"hanging notes\")."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a real-time MIDI Transpose and Velocity Scaling device that safely processes Note-On and Note-Off events without hanging notes."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI Track, insert an instance of <strong>Drift</strong> or <strong>Operator</strong>, and insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> before it.","Select <strong>Edit in Max</strong> on the Max MIDI Effect.","Instantiate a <code>[notein]</code> object.","Outlet 1: Pitch (0–127)","Outlet 2: Velocity (0–127, where 0 = Note-Off)","Outlet 3: MIDI Channel (1–16)","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>Transpose</code> (Int, range: <code>-24 to +24</code>, default: <code>0</code>).","Create a <code>[live.dial]</code> named <code>Vel_Scale</code> (Float, range: <code>0.1 to 2.0</code>, default: <code>1.0</code>).","Transpose Pitch:","Connect the pitch outlet of <code>[notein]</code> to the left inlet of a <code>[+ 0]</code> addition object.","Connect <code>[live.dial Transpose]</code> to the right inlet of <code>[+ 0]</code>.","Scale Velocity (Only for sounding notes &gt; 0):","Connect the velocity outlet of <code>[notein]</code> to a <code>[select 0]</code> object.","Outlet 2 (non-zero velocity): route through a <code>[* 1.0]</code> multiplier connected to <code>[live.dial Vel_Scale]</code>, then through <code>[clip 1 127]</code>.","Outlet 1 (zero velocity / note-off): pass <code>0</code> directly through to preserve note-off integrity.","Connect the processed pitch, velocity, and channel streams into a <code>[noteout]</code> object.","Save the device as <code>MIDI_Transpose_Scaler.amxd</code> in your User Library.","Play MIDI notes into the track in Live. Verify that notes transpose in real time and velocity dynamics scale accurately without stuck notes."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                    +---------------------------+\n                    |          notein           |\n                    +----+-------+---------+----+\n                  (Pitch)|       |(Velocity)|(Chan)\n                         |       |          |\n      +------------------+       |          +------------------+\n      |                          v                             |\n      |                    +-----------+                       |\n      |                    | select 0  |                       |\n      |                    +-----+-----+                       |\n      |             (Non-Zero Vel)|     |(Note-Off: 0)         |\n      |                           v     |                      |\n      |                    +-----------+|                      |\n      |                    |  * 1.0    ||                      |\n      |                    +-----+-----+|                      |\n      |                          v      |                      |\n      |                    +-----------+|                      |\n      |                    |clip 1 127 ||                      |\n      |                    +-----+-----+|                      |\n      |                          |      |                      |\n      |                          +---+--+                      |\n      |                              |                         |\n      v                              v                         v\n+-----------+                  +-----------+             +-----------+\n|   + 0     |                  | Velocity  |             |  Channel  |\n+-----+-----+                  +-----+-----+             +-----+-----+\n      |                              |                         |\n      v                              v                         v\n+-----+------------------------------+-------------------------+-----+\n|                              noteout                               |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Velocity Scaling Note-Off to Zero Disruption:</strong> Multiplying Note-Off events (velocity 0) by a fractional scaling factor. 0 × 0.5 = 0, which is safe, but multiplying by an offset (0 + 10 = 10) converts Note-Off events into Note-On events, causing permanent sound generation.","<strong>Channel Stripping:</strong> Omitting the channel connection between <code>[notein]</code> and <code>[noteout]</code>. This forces all outgoing MIDI onto Channel 1, breaking multi-timbral and MPE setups.","<strong>Misplacement in Signal Chain:</strong> Attempting to place a Max MIDI Effect <em>after</em> an instrument on a track. Ableton Live's device chain prohibits MIDI effects downstream of audio-generating instruments."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the three structural outlets of <code>[notein]</code> and describe the significance of a velocity <code>0</code> event.","[ ] Explain why a transposition calculation must be applied identically to both Note-On and Note-Off events.","[ ] State the device chain positioning constraint governing Max MIDI Effects inside Ableton Live."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Creating MIDI Effect Devices in Max for Live</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Section 31.3: \"Creating MIDI Effects\", pp. 585–586. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: MIDI Event Processing</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-4-3-3","code":"4.3","title":"4.3","blurb":"4.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-4-3-1","title":"4.3.1: Instrument Device Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 4.3.1 (Includes Gate C Assessment)","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 4 / Section 4.3","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 4.2.1 - MIDI Device Architecture and Flow.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Section 31.3 (<em>Creating Instruments</em>).","Cycling '74: <em>Creating Audio Devices: Instruments in Max for Live</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Instrument Signal Paths</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Explain the hybrid architecture of a Max Instrument (MIDI message ingestion at the input boundary, stereo audio generation at the output boundary).","Translate MIDI note numbers (0–127) into exact acoustic frequencies in Hertz using the <code>mtof</code> (MIDI-to-Frequency) conversion object.","Construct a monophonic synthesis voice utilizing an oscillator (<code>cycle~</code> / <code>saw~</code>), an ADSR amplitude envelope generator (<code>adsr~</code>), and a VCA multiplier stage (<code>[*~]</code>).","Prevent digital overs and clipping through proper gain staging prior to <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Complete <strong>Gate C (Template Mastery)</strong> by creating, saving, and verifying three standalone device types across all standard User Library directories."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 The Max Instrument Boundary","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A Max Instrument occupies a unique structural position in Ableton Live: it accepts a stream of MIDI messages from the track input and emits stereo audio signals to downstream audio effects and the track mixer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         HOST INSTRUMENT ROUTING GRAPH                         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  Live Track Signal Flow:                                                      |\n|  [ MIDI Clips / MIDI Controller Input ]                                       |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               | (MIDI Event Stream)                                           |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | Max Instrument (.amxd)                                                  |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |   notein / midiin |  &lt;-- Ingests MIDI control messages               |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            | (MIDI Messages: Pitch, Velocity, Gates)                    |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |  Sound Synthesis  |  &lt;-- Translates MIDI to Audio (mtof, cycle~,     |  |\n|  |  |  &amp; Envelope Engine|      adsr~, VCA gain staging)                    |  |\n|  |  +---------+---------+                                                  |  |\n|  |            |                                                            |  |\n|  |            | (Stereo MSP Audio Stream)                                  |  |\n|  |            v                                                            |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |  |     plugout~      |  &lt;-- Emits stereo audio into Live's audio chain  |  |\n|  |  +-------------------+                                                  |  |\n|  |                                                                         |  |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|               |                                                               |\n|               v                                                               |\n|  [ Audio Effects / Track Volume Fader ]                                       |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Structural Rule:</em> A Max Instrument does not use <code>[plugin~]</code>. Audio generation begins internally and terminates at <code>[plugout~]</code>."},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Pitch Translation: The mtof Object","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI encodes pitch as integer semitones (0 = C_-1, 60 = Middle C, 127 = G_9). Audio oscillators require frequency in Hertz (f). The <code>[mtof]</code> (MIDI-to-Frequency) object executes standard equal-temperament tuning calculation:"},{"type":"prose","html":"f = 440 × 2^ d - 6912"},{"type":"prose","html":"Where d is the incoming MIDI note number."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       [60] (MIDI Note Number)\n         |\n         v\n     +-------+\n     | mtof  |\n     +---+---+\n         |\n         v\n     [261.625565 Hz] (Frequency Float)</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.3 Dynamic Enveloping with adsr~","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To shape sound duration and dynamics, incoming velocity triggers an amplitude envelope:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>[adsr~ &lt;attack_ms&gt; &lt;decay_ms&gt; &lt;sustain_level&gt; &lt;release_ms&gt;]</code>:</strong> Generates an audio-rate control envelope (0.0 to 1.0).","When non-zero velocity arrives → <code>adsr~</code> enters Attack → Decay → Sustain.","When velocity <code>0</code> (Note-Off) arrives → <code>adsr~</code> enters Release."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       Velocity (0-127)\n             |\n             v\n       +-----------+\n       |   &gt; 0     |  (Gate: 1 on Note-On, 0 on Note-Off)\n       +-----+-----+\n             |\n             v\n     +---------------+\n     | adsr~ 10 100  |\n     |       0.7 200 |\n     +-------+-------+\n             |\n             | === (Envelope Signal: 0.0 - 1.0)\n             v\n       +-----------+\n       |   *~      |  &lt;-- VCA Multiplier\n       +-----------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Gain Staging and Output Limiting","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A synthesizer voice must be calibrated to nominal DAW operating levels:"},{"type":"list","items":["Full-scale sine waves generated at unity amplitude (1.0) peak at 0 dBFS.","When summing harmonics or multiple oscillators, internal levels exceed 1.0, creating harsh digital clipping at <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Always scale raw oscillator output by a master gain stage (e.g., <code>-12\\text{ dB}</code> to <code>-6\\text{ dB}</code> headroom) before output."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a monophonic synthesizer instrument with pitch tracking, an ADSR envelope, filter tone control, and master gain attenuation."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create an empty MIDI Track, insert a default <strong>Max Instrument</strong>, and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Instantiate <code>[notein]</code> to receive MIDI from Live.","Route the pitch outlet (outlet 1) to <code>[mtof]</code>.","Connect the output of <code>[mtof]</code> to the frequency inlet of <code>[cycle~]</code> (Oscillator).","Route the velocity outlet (outlet 2) of <code>[notein]</code> to a <code>[&gt; 0]</code> comparison object to produce a binary gate (<code>1</code> for Note-On, <code>0</code> for Note-Off).","Create an envelope generator: <code>[adsr~ 15 150 0.6 300]</code>. Connect the gate from <code>[&gt; 0]</code> to the inlet of <code>[adsr~]</code>.","Create a VCA multiplier: <code>[*~ 0.0]</code>.","Connect the output of <code>[cycle~]</code> to inlet 1 of <code>[*~]</code>.","Connect the envelope output of <code>[adsr~]</code> to inlet 2 of <code>[*~]</code>.","Create a master gain stage: <code>[*~ 0.3]</code> (scales signal to prevent clipping).","Connect the output of <code>[<em>~ 0.0]</code> (VCA) through <code>[</em>~ 0.3]</code> to both inlets of <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Save the device as <code>Monophonic_Sine_Synth.amxd</code> in <code>User Library/Presets/Instruments/Max Instrument/</code>.","Return to Live, arm the MIDI track, and play notes from an external keyboard or MIDI clip. Verify clean, polyphony-free monophonic playback with smooth release tails."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                    +---------------------------+\n                    |          notein           |\n                    +----+-----------------+----+\n                  (Pitch)|                 |(Velocity)\n                         v                 v\n                   +-----------+     +-----------+\n                   |   mtof    |     |    &gt; 0    | (Gate: 1/0)\n                   +-----+-----+     +-----+-----+\n                         |                 |\n                         v                 v\n                   +-----------+     +-------------------+\n                   |  cycle~   |     | adsr~ 15 150 .6 300|\n                   +-----+-----+     +---------+---------+\n                         | (Oscillator)        | (Env Signal)\n                         v                     v\n                   +---------------------------+\n                   |            *~             | (VCA Stage)\n                   +-------------+-------------+\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                           +-----------+\n                           |  *~ 0.3   | (Master Gain Headroom)\n                           +-----+-----+\n                                 |\n                                 +-----------------+\n                                 |                 |\n                                 v                 v\n                           +-----------------------------+\n                           |          plugout~           |\n                           +-----------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Gate C Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!IMPORTANT] <strong>Gate C: Template Mastery Assessment</strong> To complete Module 4 and verify foundational I/O architecture across all device families, the student must execute and verify the following three builds:"},{"type":"heading","text":"Practical Test Assignment:","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Max Audio Effect (<code>.amxd</code>):</strong>","Built from template with <code>[plugin~]</code> → dual gain control → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Saved to: <code>User Library/Presets/Audio Effects/Max Audio Effect/</code>.","<strong>Max MIDI Effect (<code>.amxd</code>):</strong>","Built from template with <code>[notein]</code> → octave transposer (+12) with Note-Off safety → <code>[noteout]</code>.","Saved to: <code>User Library/Presets/MIDI Effects/Max MIDI Effect/</code>.","<strong>Max Instrument (<code>.amxd</code>):</strong>","Built from template with <code>[notein]</code> → <code>[mtof]</code> → <code>[cycle~]</code> → <code>[adsr~]</code> → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Saved to: <code>User Library/Presets/Instruments/Max Instrument/</code>.","<strong>Verification:</strong>","Create a clean Live Set.","Drag all three devices from the User Library into the Set.","Verify that all three pass signal and control without errors or missing references."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Placing <code>plugin~</code> inside a Max Instrument:</strong> Inserting <code>[plugin~]</code> into an instrument patch. An instrument receives MIDI from the track header, not audio. <code>plugin~</code> will remain silent.","<strong>Stuck Audio from Missing Release Gate:</strong> Driving an oscillator without an envelope or gate. When a MIDI note ends, the oscillator continues outputting a constant tone indefinitely.","<strong>Direct Velocity-to-Frequency Routing:</strong> Connecting velocity (loudness) into <code>mtof</code> or frequency inlets. Velocity must modulate amplitude, while pitch modulates frequency."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Section 31.3: \"Creating Instruments\", pp. 586–587. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Creating Audio Devices: Instruments in Max for Live</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Instrument Design Rules</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
